WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.470
A supplier told our district their reading

2
00:00:03.470 --> 00:00:05.910
app is ninety-five per cent accurate.

3
00:00:06.710 --> 00:00:10.270
My first question was: accurate for whom?

4
00:00:11.550 --> 00:00:14.390
Today: audit an AI tool before

5
00:00:14.390 --> 00:00:15.590
your school trusts it.

6
00:00:16.770 --> 00:00:18.650
The app listens to children reading

7
00:00:18.650 --> 00:00:20.370
aloud and marks each word.

8
00:00:21.030 --> 00:00:23.570
But ninety-five per cent is an average.

9
00:00:24.440 --> 00:00:26.340
Tested mostly on city children,

10
00:00:26.840 --> 00:00:28.680
it can fail village children and

11
00:00:28.680 --> 00:00:30.180
still score ninety-five.

12
00:00:30.900 --> 00:00:32.740
Voice tools often struggle with

13
00:00:32.740 --> 00:00:34.480
accents rare in their training.

14
00:00:35.680 --> 00:00:37.580
So plan a small audit.

15
00:00:38.290 --> 00:00:40.870
Choose the groups to compare: girls and

16
00:00:40.870 --> 00:00:43.970
boys, town and village, home language.

17
00:00:44.680 --> 00:00:46.820
Collect about twenty real

18
00:00:46.820 --> 00:00:48.320
samples from each group.

19
00:00:48.930 --> 00:00:50.650
A teacher marks them, and

20
00:00:50.650 --> 00:00:51.410
so does the tool.

21
00:00:51.990 --> 00:00:54.070
Then count its errors for each

22
00:00:54.070 --> 00:00:55.930
group: right answers marked

23
00:00:55.930 --> 00:00:58.070
wrong, and mistakes missed.

24
00:00:59.330 --> 00:01:02.370
An AI audit is a planned test on

25
00:01:02.370 --> 00:01:04.350
work like your pupils’, comparing

26
00:01:04.350 --> 00:01:06.050
errors group by group.

27
00:01:06.690 --> 00:01:09.490
Twenty samples is small, so look

28
00:01:09.490 --> 00:01:11.450
for big gaps, not tiny ones.

29
00:01:12.700 --> 00:01:14.060
It’s like new seed.

30
00:01:14.770 --> 00:01:16.430
The packet promises a great

31
00:01:16.430 --> 00:01:18.590
harvest, grown somewhere else.

32
00:01:19.220 --> 00:01:21.780
A farmer sows one test row

33
00:01:21.780 --> 00:01:24.000
first, beside her usual crop.

34
00:01:24.580 --> 00:01:26.540
Your pupils are your soil.

35
00:01:27.830 --> 00:01:28.230
Quick check!

36
00:01:28.850 --> 00:01:30.850
A tool is ninety-five per

37
00:01:30.850 --> 00:01:31.850
cent accurate overall.

38
00:01:32.640 --> 00:01:34.100
What should you check next?

39
00:01:35.430 --> 00:01:37.050
Its errors for each group.

40
00:01:37.660 --> 00:01:40.740
A bigger test still gives one average, and

41
00:01:40.740 --> 00:01:42.320
that’s where a failing group hides.

42
00:01:43.080 --> 00:01:45.560
Ask for results per group, and if

43
00:01:45.560 --> 00:01:47.440
nobody has them, make them yourself.

44
00:01:48.820 --> 00:01:51.440
Before any pilot, ask the supplier.

45
00:01:52.200 --> 00:01:54.920
Whose data trained it, in which languages?

46
00:01:55.730 --> 00:01:57.550
Who was it tested on, with

47
00:01:57.550 --> 00:01:58.730
what results per group?

48
00:01:59.260 --> 00:02:01.360
Where do pupils’ recordings go?

49
00:02:02.040 --> 00:02:04.420
Does it work offline, on cheap phones?

50
00:02:05.180 --> 00:02:07.920
And what will it cost after the free year?

51
00:02:09.170 --> 00:02:10.790
Then decide from the evidence.

52
00:02:11.660 --> 00:02:13.200
Adopt it when errors are

53
00:02:13.200 --> 00:02:14.560
low for every group.

54
00:02:15.190 --> 00:02:17.490
Pilot it in one class for a term

55
00:02:17.490 --> 00:02:18.690
when the evidence is mixed.

56
00:02:19.360 --> 00:02:22.180
Refuse it when a group fails or

57
00:02:22.180 --> 00:02:23.700
the supplier won’t answer.

58
00:02:24.360 --> 00:02:26.380
Either way, a teacher checks its

59
00:02:26.380 --> 00:02:29.120
decisions, and pupils can ask for a second

60
00:02:29.120 --> 00:02:29.380
look.

61
00:02:30.590 --> 00:02:32.310
In the app, you’ll audit a

62
00:02:32.310 --> 00:02:34.030
leaf-disease spotter trained

63
00:02:34.030 --> 00:02:36.050
mostly on sunny-valley photos.

64
00:02:36.860 --> 00:02:38.960
Overall, it’s wrong only

65
00:02:38.960 --> 00:02:40.540
three times in a hundred.

66
00:02:41.180 --> 00:02:43.040
But in the cloudy hills, it

67
00:02:43.040 --> 00:02:45.160
misses one sick leaf in three.

68
00:02:45.790 --> 00:02:47.670
Add hill photos to its training,

69
00:02:48.010 --> 00:02:49.650
and watch the gap close.

70
00:02:50.980 --> 00:02:51.500
Your turn.

71
00:02:52.130 --> 00:02:55.410
Our audit: the app wrongly marks village

72
00:02:55.410 --> 00:02:58.610
readers six times as often, and the

73
00:02:58.610 --> 00:03:00.750
supplier hides its training data.

74
00:03:01.420 --> 00:03:04.440
Adopt, pilot or refuse?

75
00:03:05.950 --> 00:03:08.330
Refuse it, for now: a group fails,

76
00:03:08.870 --> 00:03:10.190
and the supplier won’t answer.

77
00:03:10.800 --> 00:03:14.220
Remember: test on work like your pupils’,

78
00:03:14.300 --> 00:03:16.880
count errors per group, ask hard

79
00:03:16.880 --> 00:03:19.180
questions, and decide from evidence.

80
00:03:20.550 --> 00:03:21.750
Try it in class tomorrow:

81
00:03:21.850 --> 00:03:23.370
“Test the tester”.

82
00:03:23.840 --> 00:03:25.800
Pupils take turns reading one

83
00:03:25.800 --> 00:03:28.140
sentence into voice typing, in

84
00:03:28.140 --> 00:03:29.780
their own voices and languages.

85
00:03:30.610 --> 00:03:33.070
On the board, they count its mistakes for

86
00:03:33.070 --> 00:03:35.570
each speaker, and discuss where it fails.
